\chapter{Explicit Context-Free Grammars and Earley Parsing}

\texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G} separates grammar productions from lexing. A user lexer produces a sequence of string tokens; named productions describe how those tokens form an AST. The Earley parser handles left recursion, nullable productions and unordered alternatives.

\section{Symbols and productions}

\texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l} distinguishes a terminal token from a nonterminal name. \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} stores a left-hand-side name and a right-hand-side symbol sequence. \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G} stores \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t} and \texttt{p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s} and offers \texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d}, \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} and \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
dsl::CFG grammar;
grammar.start = "Expr";
grammar.add("Expr", {dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal("Expr"),
                     dsl::CFGSymbol::token("+"),
                     dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal("Term")});
grammar.add("Expr", {dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal("Term")});
grammar.add("Term", {dsl::CFGSymbol::token("number")});
grammar.validate();
assert(grammar.parse({"number", "+", "number"}).ok());
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
CFG grammar;
grammar.start = "Expr";
grammar.add("Expr", [CFGSymbol.nonterminal("Expr"),
                     CFGSymbol.token("+"), CFGSymbol.nonterminal("Term")]);
grammar.add("Expr", [CFGSymbol.nonterminal("Term")]);
grammar.add("Term", [CFGSymbol.token("number")]);
grammar.validate();
assert(grammar.parse(["number", "+", "number"]).ok());
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Terminals compare exactly against string tokens. \texttt{"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}"} in this example is a literal token value supplied by the lexer; it is not a built-in token class or regex. The user's lexer can normalize numeric input to that token, or construct a grammar using its own concrete token values.

Validation requires nonempty production names, a defined start symbol, and definitions for every referenced nonterminal. \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e} validates before running.

\section{Read the grammar from S-expressions}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{lisp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
(grammar
    (start Expr)
    (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term))
    (rule Term ("number")))
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto grammar = dsl::sexpr_grammar(R"((grammar
    (start Expr)
    (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term))
    (rule Term ("number"))))");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto grammar = sexpr_grammar(`(grammar
    (start Expr)
    (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term))
    (rule Term ("number")))`);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The declaration grammar is:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{ebnf}{}
\begin{MDCode}
grammar = "(grammar" , start , { rule } , ")" ;
start = "(start" , name , ")" ;
rule = "(rule" , name , alternative , { alternative } , ")" ;
alternative = "(" , { symbol } , ")" ;
symbol = nonterminal_name | quoted_terminal ;
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The notation permits declaration ordering independent of references: all references are validated after construction. Unquoted RHS atoms are nonterminals; quoted RHS atoms are terminals. Each RHS list is a separate alternative. An empty list is an epsilon production.

\section{Use template-level vocabulary}

\texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}>}, and \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}H\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}>} provide C++ template construction. D uses native string arguments and \texttt{!}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
using Number = dsl::CFGProduction<"Term", dsl::CFGTerminal<"number">>;
using Expression = dsl::CFGProduction<"Expr", dsl::CFGNonterminal<"Term">>;
auto grammar = dsl::make_cfg<"Expr">(Expression{}, Number{});
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
alias Number = CFGProduction!("Term", CFGTerminal!"number");
alias Expression = CFGProduction!("Expr", CFGNonterminal!"Term");
auto grammar = make_cfg!"Expr"(Expression(), Number());
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}g} materializes and validates a \texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G}; recognition still runs over runtime tokens. A production with no symbol types represents epsilon.

\section{Recognition and result ASTs}

Earley chart processing uses prediction, scanning and completion. An item records its production, dot position and origin token position. Equivalent chart items are deduplicated, allowing recursive and nullable grammars to reach a finite chart closure. A completed item carries the first discovered tree.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto parsed = grammar.parse({"number"});
assert(parsed.ok());
assert(parsed.tree->tag() == "Expr");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto parsed = grammar.parse(["number"]);
assert(parsed.ok());
assert(parsed.tree.get.tag() == "Expr");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{C\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t} contains an optional \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}e}, a token \texttt{p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n}, expected terminal strings, and \texttt{o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. On success, \texttt{p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n} equals the input token count. Production names become inner-node tags; terminal leaves use tag \texttt{t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n} and carry the token's text. No implicit semantic evaluation occurs.

For ambiguous grammars, the parser returns the first discovered derivation. It does not expose a packed forest of all parses. Use grammar design and production order deliberately when the selected derivation matters.

\section{Nullable productions and diagnostics}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{lisp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
(grammar
    (start S)
    (rule S (A A))
    (rule A () ("a")))
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

This grammar accepts zero, one or two \texttt{a} tokens. The parser handles completion of nullable symbols even when an appropriate waiting item is inserted after the nullable completion was first discovered.

For the expression grammar above, input \texttt{n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}+} reaches token position 2 and expects terminal \texttt{n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r}. \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d} contains distinct terminal names from the farthest populated chart. Trailing input can report \texttt{<\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}>} when no ordinary terminal expectation explains it. Undefined grammar symbols raise a validation exception; token recognition failure is a result value.

