The same S-expression substrate can hold either equality rules or CFG declarations, but their readers and semantics are separate. \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} reads rewrite rules; \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r} reads grammar declarations. A static byte regex (Chapter 33) can assist a user lexer; it is not a CFG nonterminal.

\section{Assemble the language pipeline}

A practical progression through the toolkit is:

\begin{enumerate}
\item{} Read source through a user I/\allowbreak{}O backend.
\item{} Preprocess rule/\allowbreak{}grammar source and resolve user includes or Lua-generated text.
\item{} Read the grammar and tokenize the domain source with the user's lexer.
\item{} Recognize tokens and obtain a tagged AST.
\item{} Derive a visitor for domain validation, evaluation or lowering.
\item{} Convert an expression AST to an e-graph and saturate S-expression equalities.
\item{} Trigger domain rewriters on equality and extract a representative.
\item{} Serialize the resulting representation with the user's codec/\allowbreak{}output backend.
\end{enumerate}

The reusable stages are documented in Chapters 32–35; domain vocabulary, lexing, semantic operations and backend syntax are supplied by the user.
